{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/1361778-speculative-game-design-tier-3-talents","id":1361778,"title":"[Speculative Game Design] Tier 3 Talents","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-01-18T12:26:47-08:00","id":1361778,"title":"[Speculative Game Design] Tier 3 Talents","content":"<span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Tier 3 Talents. We know at least </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>one</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> of them exists, in the form of Maxwell Mercer, a time-traveler with an unusually long lifespan. But we have no real mechanics for them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So, naturally, I'm going to try and make some up. But first, we need to set a baseline for what it takes to ascend from Tier 2, where Talents </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>normally</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> reside, to Tier 3. And the only real example we have for that are Psions. So... let's look at them a bit more closely, shall we?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">PART 1: From Psion To Proxy</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">First, let's establish what it takes for a Psion to become a Tier 3, Proxy-Tier Psion. Fortunately, we have that spelled out quite clearly for us in the Aeon corebook, on Page 209. You first have to meet the following three criterions:</span></span><ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Psi score of 5</li> <li>5 dots in one Mode</li> <li>At least 3 dots in another Mode</li> </ol><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Then, and only then, can you purchase the sixth dot of Psi, for the princely sum of 24 xp.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So, let's take a look at two psions built in opposite ways, and then calculate the xp xpenditure required to raise them to Tier 3. We'll start with a Psion built in the least optimal(</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>for this purpose</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">) manner, and then the next one will be built in the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>most</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> optimal(again, </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>for this purpose</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">) manner.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Alice</span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> is a Biokinetic. She could be any kind of psion, save for a Quantakinetic or Teleporter, but I like Biokinetics, and also they come first, alphabetically, so Alice will be a Biokinetic. Since we only really care about her Psi score and Mode dots, let's deal with those first.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">A starting Psion has four Mode dots to distribute, and only three of them </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>have</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> to go into Primary Modes. So, let's say Alice has a single dot in all three of Biokinesis' Modes, and her fourth dot went into... oh, let's say Perikinesis, so she could have forcefields. Those are always useful. As for Alice's Psi score, she has the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>option</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> of raising it to 3, but that would require giving up the benefit of an extra Attribute point, which she doesn't want to do, so it stays at 2.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now, to get Alice to Tier 3, first we need to raise her Psi from 2 to 5, which means purchasing three dots of it; fortunately, the Storypath system uses completely flat xp costs, which makes the math easier. Psi costs 12 xp per dot up to Psi 5, and so Alice would have to spend 36 xp in total to raise her Psi to 5.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Next, we need to raise Alice's Modes some. Let's say she </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>really</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> likes Transmogrify, thinks Adaptation is pretty useful, and could take or leave Psychomorphing. So, to raise Transmogrify from 1 to 5, she has to buy four dots, at 6 xp per dot, for a subtotal of 24 xp. Now she has to raise Adaptation from 1 to 3, which only requires two dots, coming out to a subtotal of 12 xp, and a total of 36 xp on Mode dots.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Alice is now finally ready to buy her sixth dot of Psi, for a princely sum of 24 xp. That, on top of the 72 xp she's already spent on Psi and Mode Dots, brings her total xp xpenditure to 96 xp.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So, let's look at Bella, who's much more optimized- once more, </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>for this purpose</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Bella is a Quantakinetic, solely because I think they're cooler than Teleporters. She </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>has</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> to trade her extra Attribute dot for another Psi dot, making her Psi 3. Furthermore, she spends all 4 of her starting Mode dots on just </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>two</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Modes- let's say Fundamentals gets 3 dots, and Transmutation gets 1, because she's a fan of Fullmetal Alchemist.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Bella only needs to raise her Psi by </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>two</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> points, which only costs 24 xp. She also only really needs to raise Transmutation from 1 to 5, because she's </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>already</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>got</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> 3 dots in Fundamentals; raising a Mode by 4 dots still costs 24 xp, but that's the end of it, because </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>now</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, she already qualifies for that sixth dot of Psi, at 24 more xp.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Her total expenditure to get to Tier 3 was 72; by the time Alice starts saving up for Psi 6, Bella has already achieved it, and is doing proxy-level shit, and also making gold out of nothing, which isn't really the most effective use of her powers but </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>does</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> make her happy.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Now, while Alice and Bella bicker about character builds before going on a life-changing space adventure that ends with a newfound respect and also a coffee date at a lovely little hole in the wall in New Venice, which is on Europa, </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>we</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> are going to discuss some numbers. 96 xp is the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>maximum</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> xp xpenditure to ascend to Tier 3- sure, characters can spend </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>more</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> than that without getting to Tier 3, but mostly by spending it on stuff we've already acknowledged we aren't going to pay attention to. And 72 xp is the </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>minimum</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> xp xpenditure to reach Tier 3.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So, if we're going to use that as a benchmark for how </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Talents</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> reach Tier 3, we're gonna have to massage some numbers such that they come out to be </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>roughly</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> within the same range, which we'll just round off to 70-100xp, for conveniences' sake.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">PART 2: It's A Long Way To The Top If You Wanna Rock And Roll</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Talents are a little bit different from Psions. Their main power stat, Inspiration, ranges from 1 to 10, and isn't purchased directly. Instead, Talents have three power sub-stats called Facets. Every odd-numbered Facet dot- and Facets have dot ratings from 0 to 5- has the added benefit of increasing Inspiration by 1. So, unless you're putting the very first dot in a Facet, Inspiration </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>pretty much</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> costs two Facet dots, which themselves cost 10 xp each.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Don't worry if you don't understand, we'll go through a pair of examples.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Caden is a new Talent; like all Talents at character creation, she has three dots to allocate amongst her Facets however she wishes. Wanting a strong Facet right out of the gate, she dumps all three points into her Intuitive Facet, leaving her with an Inspiration of 3.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Dahlia is </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>also</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> a new Talent; however, she spreads her three Facet dots out evenly among her three Facets, giving her an Inspiration of 4.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">And now, with the application of 20 xp towards Facet dots aimed at maximizing Inspiration, it is </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>entirely possible</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">- in fact, a 1 in 3 chance- for Caden and Dahlia to end up with exactly the same Facet dots allocation of Destructive 1, Intuitive 3, and Reflective 1. Talents cannot really be optimized for quick Inspiration growth, as you can see. Even if we suppose Eartha put two dots in Intuitive and one in Reflective, 20 xp spent to optimize Inspiration growth will </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>still</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> produce D1,I3,R1.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So, now let's figure out how much it costs to get Inspiration 10. Let's use Dahlia's loadout, simply because it makes the math easier, but the numbers are the same for all of them; upgrading a Facet from 1 to 5 costs 40 xp and grants +2 Inspiration. Do that three times, and Dahlia's spent 120 xp to get Inspiration 10. That's a decent chunk </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>more</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> than what even an anti-optimized Psion has to spend, and while &quot;Talents hit Tier 3 at Inspiration 10&quot; is certainly an </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>easy</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> ruling to make, since it's a nice round number, I don't think I much care for it. So, let's look at some different numbers.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">For Dahlia, it's pretty easy to say that 20 xp will purchase 1 dot of Inspiration; that it also gets two Facet dots is nice, but not immediately relevant. Pegging the xp target at 80, since it's nicely between 70 and 100, we get Inspiration 8 as our target for Tier 3 Talents, which will, incidentally, pretty much </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>require</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> that at least one of the Talent's Facets is maxed out. This </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>also</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> gives a bit of wiggle room in Tier 3 for Talents- see, Tier 3 Psions can be Psi 6 </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>or</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Psi 7, which allows for mild power gradients within the Tier, which I think is nice to have. And with 3 different levels of Inspiration a Talent can have to attain Tier 3, that produces some power gradients of its own, which I like.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">But, well, there's </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>other</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> options to explore. Options that could be much more interesting, and produce more interesting </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>characters</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, than just &quot;max out Inspiration.&quot;</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Let's go back to Caden, who dumped all 3 of her Facet dots into Intuitive. It would only take her 20xp to max out that Facet; Dahlia, meanwhile, would require 40 xp to max out any Facet in particular. So, let's say that the Inspiration side of the Tier 3 requirements are simply &quot;5 dots in at least one Facet,&quot; and look at some </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>other</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, more </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>interesting</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> things you can xpend xp on.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Psions required not </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>just</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Psi 5 to ascend, but </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>also</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> 5 dots in one Mode and at least 3 dots in another. However, Talents don't </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>have</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Modes. They have </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Gifts</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, which aren't arranged in ranked chains of progressively stronger but also closely related powers, but are instead just a collection of miscellaneous perks one can pick and choose from as one pleases. Still, they're the main way a Talent upgrades and expresses the state of </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>being </i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">a Talent, so let's run with them.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Talents start with 4 Gifts, right out of the gate, and can purchase more for 5 xp apiece. So, to come up with a range of xp totals that roughly matches up with 72-96, let's say Talents need, in addition to a maxed-out Facet, a total of... seventy minus twenty, fifty, divided by five, plus four... 14 Gifts in order- no, </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>15</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Gifts, because that's a rounder, nicer number than 14. 15 Gifts to reach Tier 3. That puts the range of xp xpenditure at 75 xp in the best case to 95 xp in the worst case, which matches up </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>very</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> closely with the Psion's range.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So, there's our two options for &quot;how do Talents reach Tier 3?&quot;:</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">1) Attain Inspiration 8(80 xp).</span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">2) Reach 5 dots in any one Facet, along with 15 Gifts(75-95 xp).</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Having thought about it some...</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">...personally, I think I just like the first one better. Gifts are certainly </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>useful</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, but one of the best sources of raw </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>power</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Talents have is Dramatic Editing, which is powered by Inspiration points. Having more Inspiration lets you use more Dramatic Editing, which makes you more powerful. Meanwhile, if our only Inspired criteria is having five dots in one Facet, then some punk could pour all their points into just one Facet and hit Tier 3 with only Inspiration 4, which kinda feels wrong. So, from here on, I'm going to ignore that second option, which was a nice intellectual exercise, but probably isn't a very good criteria to use in actual play.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Anyhow, now that we've established </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>how</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> Talents hit Tier 3, let's maybe noodle around with what that means.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">PART 3: Tier Three Talents</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So, our only canon example of a Tier 3 Talent is, of course, Max Mercer, a long-lived time traveler. &quot;Time travel&quot; is, therefore, something that a Tier 3 Talent should, in theory, be capable of. Maybe it's a high-level power even for Tier 3 Talents... and, incidentally, we should come up with a name for them, just for the sake of convenience. So... let's call them Heroes, after some old terminology; in Adventure, what we'd now call Gifts were instead called &quot;Heroic Knacks.&quot; It's not very original, I'll grant, but it's just a working title, and it's better than my original idea of simply calling them Paramorphs, after the old technical term for Talents/Daredevils. Also, let's call the powers available to Heroes &quot;Heroic Knacks,&quot; simply because it's a perfectly good term.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So, let's assume Time Travel is the highest, capstone Heroic Knack, requiring Inspiration 10 to purchase. The mechanics of how it works aren't entirely germane at the moment, although that won't stop me from saying &quot;treat it like the Clairsentience Capstone power, Destiny, except it can also do stuff that could reasonably be done with time travel, provided the player can justify it.&quot; What </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>does</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> matter is figuring out what it </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>means</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> to be a Hero, and what they, generally, </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>do</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">So, let's go back and look at the canonical technobabble for how Talents work. In essence, they utilize parallel universes in subtle ways, through the energy that exists </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>between</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> parallel universes called Flux. Talents use it in subtle ways to minorly change circumstances in their favor, so therefore, Heroes should use it in much more blatant and weird ways to change things much more. For example, Max Mercer can use Flux to time travel, which can be explained with parallel universes in any number of ways. So, what are some </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>other</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> ways for Heroes to utilize Flux in more blatant ways?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Well, what about jumping between universes at will? It enables </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>some</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> of the shenanigans of Time Travel, too, such as &quot;going back to Ancient Rome and stabbing Caesar yourself,&quot; but not </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>all</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> of them- since it wasn't </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>your</i></span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"> universe you did that to, you can't actually change your 'real' history with it. It can also enable adventures in the mode of, say, Sliders or Rick And Morty, traveling between alternate universes and seeing whatever weird bullshit is going on there.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Or how about teleporting within the same world, as an extension of earlier world-hopping, and as a prelude to time-travel; you're getting better and better at unsticking yourself from the universe in specific ways.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Or, to go in a different direction, how about something like Clairsentience? It starts with stuff like intuitive clairvoyance, with a side order of being able to spot Flux-touched people at a glance, and then upgrades to... Iunno, being able to predict the future.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Maybe Heroes can do Powered Science, just like Psions and Novas can. Doc Brown seems like he could be a Hero, why not?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">There's a lot of potential powers in the fictive arena of &quot;My powers work by manipulating parallel universes and the boundaries between them.&quot; Feel free to supply your own ideas for what Heroic Knacks could do, I'm barely scratching the surface, and I bet this is a fertile ground for all sorts of cool, bizarre nonsense that could, potentially, one day be usable.</span></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25892-horizonthetransient","user":"HorizonTheTransient","date":". 01-18-2020, 03:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25892-horizonthetransient","name":"HorizonTheTransient","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-18T15:07:04-08:00","id":1361792,"title":"","content":"Interesting prerequisites to become tier 3, lots of gifts would be my pick, probably with a minimum variety of skill and luck, let\u2019s say at least 3 of each.<br /> <br /> I would forget about the Inspiration value (as it\u2019s no more than the available energy) and would deal directly with the Facets. A tier 3 talent would need to have a Facet on 6 dots, limited on that probably, like a Psiad is limited to Psi 6, and an Ultimate Talent would have a limit to 7 dots in the Facets.<br /> <br /> On Super Science, the ideas are great, check In Media Res for a few powered talent items, dimension hopping is the main focus, but also teleportation and the inter dimension area (Mist).<br /> <br /> I am touching this idea in my Wuxia book... just finishing the pdf to submit... but I am a bit overcharged with my work, so it\u2019s going slow...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/481584/h